\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 RELATED SOFTWARE
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 ================
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Numerous viewing and image manipulation programs now support JPEG.  (Quite a
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 few of them use this library to do so.)  The JPEG FAQ described above lists
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 some of the more popular free and shareware viewers, and tells where to
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 obtain them on Internet.
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 If you are on a Unix machine, we highly recommend Jef Poskanzer's free
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 PBMPLUS software, which provides many useful operations on PPM-format i\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 mage
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 files.  In particular, it can convert PPM images to and from a wide range of
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 other formats, thus making cjpeg/djpeg considerably more useful.  The latest
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 version is distributed by the NetPBM group, and is available from numerous
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 sites, notably ftp://wuarchive.wustl.edu/graphics/graphics/packages/NetPBM/.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Unfortunately PBMPLUS/NETPBM is not nearly as portable as the IJG software is;
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 you are likely to have difficulty making it work on any non-Unix machine.
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 A different free JPEG implementation, written b\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 y the PVRG group at Stanford,
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 is available from ftp://havefun.stanford.edu/pub/jpeg/.  This program
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 is designed for research and experimentation rather than production use;
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 it is slower, harder to use, and less portable than the IJG code, but it
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 is easier to read and modify.  Also, the PVRG code supports lossless JPEG,
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 which we do not.  (On the other hand, it doesn't do progressive JPEG.)
\par 
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 FILE FORMAT WARS
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 ================
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Some JPEG programs produce files that are not compatible with our library.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 The root of the problem is that the ISO JPEG committee failed to specify a
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 concrete file format.  Some vendors "filled in the blanks" on their own,
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 creating proprietary formats that no one else could read.  (For example, none
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 of the early commercial JPEG implementations for the Macintosh were able to
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 exchange compressed files.)
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 The file format we have adopted is called JFIF (see REFERENCES).  This format
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 has been agreed to by a numb\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 er of major commercial JPEG vendors, and it has
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 become the de facto standard.  JFIF is a minimal or "low end" representation.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 We recommend the use of TIFF/JPEG (TIFF revision 6.0 as modified by TIFF
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Technical Note #2) for "high end" applications that need to record a lot of
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 additional data about an image.  TIFF/JPEG is fairly new and not yet widely
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 supported, unfortunately.
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 The upcoming JPEG Part 3 standard defines a file format called SPIFF.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 SPIFF is interoperable with JFIF, in the sense that most JFIF de\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 coders should
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 be able to read the most common variant of SPIFF.  SPIFF has some technical
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 advantages over JFIF, but its major claim to fame is simply that it is an
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 official standard rather than an informal one.  At this point it is unclear
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 whether SPIFF will supersede JFIF or whether JFIF will remain the de-facto
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 standard.  IJG intends to support SPIFF once the standard is frozen, but we
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 have not decided whether it should become our default output format or not.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 (In any case, our decoder will remain capable\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456  of reading JFIF indefinitely.)
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Various proprietary file formats incorporating JPEG compression also exist.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 We have little or no sympathy for the existence of these formats.  Indeed,
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 one of the original reasons for developing this free software was to help
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 force convergence on common, open format standards for JPEG files.  Don't
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 use a proprietary file format!
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 The major thrust for v7 will probably be improvement of visual quality.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 The current method for scaling the quantization tables is known not to be
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 very good at low Q values.  We also intend to investigate block boundary
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 smoothing, "poor man's variable quanti\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 zation", and other means of improving
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 quality-vs-file-size performance without sacrificing compatibility.
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 In future versions, we are considering supporting some of the upcoming JPEG
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Part 3 extensions --- principally, variable quantization and the SPIFF file
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 format.
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 As always, speeding things up is of great interest.
\par 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Please send bug reports, offers of help, etc. to jpeg-info@uunet.uu.net.
\par 
