\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 Browserlauncher
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456  BrowserLauncher is a class that provides one static method, openURL, which
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456  opens the default web browser for the current user of the system to the given
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456  URL. It may support other protocols depending on the system -- mailto, ftp,
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456  etc. -- but that has not been rigorously tested and is not guaranteed to
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456  work.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   Yes, this is platform-specific code, and yes, it may rely on classes on
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   certain platforms that are not part of the standard JDK. What we're trying to
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   do, though, is to take something that's frequently desirable but inherently
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   platform-specific -- opening a default\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456  browser -- and allow programmers (you,
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   for example) to do so without worrying about dropping into native code or
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   doing anything else similarly evil.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   Anyway, this code is completely in Java and will run on all JDK 1.1-compliant
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   systems without modification or a need for additional libraries. All classes
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   that are required on certain platforms to allow this to run are dynamically
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   loaded at runtime via reflection and, if not found, will not cause this to do
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   anything other than returning an err\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 or when opening the browser.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   There are certain system requirements for this class, as it's running through
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   Runtime.exec(), which is Java's way of making a native system call.
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   Currently, this requires that a Macintosh have a Finder which supports the
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   GURL event, which is true for Mac OS 8.0 and 8.1 systems that have the
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   Internet Scripting AppleScript dictionary installed in the Scripting
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   Additions folder in the Extensions folder (which is installed by default as
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   far as I know under Mac OS 8.\hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456 0 and 8.1), and for all Mac OS 8.5 and later
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   systems. On Windows, it only runs under Win32 systems (NT, 4.0, 
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   as well as later versions of all). On other systems, this drops back
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   from the inherently platform-sensitive concept of a default browser and
\par \hich\af1456\dbch\af31505\loch\f1456   simply attempts to launch Netscape via a shell command.
